"Surah al-A'raf begins with the disjointed letters (huruf al-muqatta'at). There are various interpretations regarding the meaning of these letters. According to a narration from Ibn Abbas, they mean 'Allah knows and explains.' Al-Suddi, on the other hand, stated that it is an abbreviation of the name 'the Fashioner' (al-Musawwir). However, commentators of the Quran point out that such interpretations are not reliable since they do not rest upon definitive evidence. These letters indicate that the surah contains allegorical (mutashabih) verses and that certain truths lie beyond human comprehension.\n\nIn the surah, it is said: 'O community of Muhammad! Follow the Book sent down to you from your Lord, and do not take protectors and helpers besides Him.' Do not follow any word or deed contrary to the Book. How many a city there was whose people were suddenly overtaken by Our punishment while they were in heedlessness! This is recalled as the earthly end of those who do not follow the Book.\n\nOn the Day of Resurrection, questions will be put both to the communities to whom prophets were sent and to the prophets themselves. This questioning will take the form of rebuke and holding to account. Since the sins of the guilty will be evident from their faces, they will not be questioned individually about them, but rather how they responded to the conveyance of the prophets' messages will be interrogated. Allah knows all things, and nothing is hidden from Him.\n\nThe weighing on that Day is the truth. Everyone's deeds will be weighed; those whose good deeds outweigh their evil deeds will attain salvation, while those whose scales are light will have ruined themselves. This is the consequence of their wronging Allah's verses and denying them.\n\nAllah established you on the earth and provided you with means of livelihood. Despite this, how little gratitude you show! Then He created you, fashioned you, and commanded the angels to prostrate before Adam. All the angels prostrated except Iblis. Iblis grew arrogant, saying, 'I am better than him; You created me from fire and created him from clay,' and refused to prostrate.\n\nAllah said to Iblis: 'Then descend from here, for it is not for you to be arrogant here. Get out; you are indeed among the degraded.' Iblis asked for respite until the Day people are resurrected. Allah granted him respite. Iblis said: 'Because You have led me astray, I will surely lie in wait for them on Your straight path; I will approach them from before them and behind them, from their right and from their left, and You will not find most of them grateful.'\n\nAllah said: 'Get out of here, disgraced and expelled. I will surely fill Hell with all of those who follow you.' He then commanded Adam and his wife to dwell in Paradise, to eat from wherever they pleased, but not to approach this tree, lest they become of the wrongdoers. Satan whispered to them in order to deceive them, saying: 'Your Lord has only forbidden you this tree lest you become angels or become immortal.' He swore to them that he was giving them sincere counsel. Thus, he deceived them, and they tasted of the tree. When their private parts became visible to them, they began to cover themselves with the leaves of Paradise. Their Lord called out to them: 'Did I not forbid you from that tree and tell you that Satan is an open enemy to you?'\n\nAdam and his wife repented, saying: 'Our Lord! We have wronged ourselves, and if You do not forgive us and have mercy upon us, we will surely be among the losers.' Allah said: 'Descend, being enemies to one another. On earth you will have a dwelling place and livelihood for a time. There you shall live, there you shall die, and from there you shall be brought forth resurrected.'\n\nIblis's refusal to prostrate stemmed from arrogance and a flawed analogy (qiyas). In saying 'I am better than him,' he took the substance of creation as his measure, while failing to take into account the spirit, form, and purpose decreed by Allah. This is the consequence of materialism and personal pride. Iblis did not deny Allah, nor did he deny resurrection after death; rather, he fell into disbelief by refusing to obey the command and by disputing it. This event serves as a warning and a means of trial for humanity.\n\nUpon being granted respite by Allah, instead of being grateful and repenting, Iblis swore that he would attack people from every direction to mislead them. He said that he would come at them from their front, from behind them, from their right, and from their left, and that He would not find most of them grateful. Thereupon, Allah expelled him from Paradise as banished and degraded, announcing that He would fill Hell with him and whoever follows him.\n\nAllah did not expel Iblis immediately, but questioned him; yet when he responded with arrogance and obstinacy, he was cast down from his station. While Iblis had the chance to regret and reform, he grew utterly defiant instead and resolved to commit evil eternally. It was then that he was completely expelled by Allah and condemned to eternal punishment along with those who follow him. The fall of Iblis in this trial that began with the creation of Adam is an admonition and lesson for humankind so that they do not fall into the same error.\n\nWhen Adam and Eve (Hawwa) were placed in Paradise, they were permitted to eat and drink as they wished, but were forbidden to approach a specific tree. This prohibition showed that they were not entirely devoid of responsibility and that this tree was a realm of trial. Satan whispered to them in order to expose their private parts, deceiving them by saying that if they ate from this tree, they would become angels or abide eternally. Tricked by Satan, who swore that he only desired their good, Adam and Eve ate from the forbidden tree, and immediately their private parts were exposed.\n\nRealizing their mistake, Adam and Eve immediately repented and pleaded with Allah: 'Our Lord, we have wronged ourselves; if You do not forgive us and show mercy to us, we will surely be among the losers.' Allah accepted their repentance, yet sent them down to earth. In this narrative, while Adam's five qualities (admitting his mistake, feeling remorse, blaming oneself, repentance, and never despairing of mercy) brought him felicity, Iblis's contrary actions (refusing to admit his mistake, feeling no remorse, not blaming himself, attributing the fault to Allah, and despairing of mercy) brought him wretchedness.\n\nAllah sent down to the Children of Adam clothing to cover their private parts and as an adornment. But the garment of piety (taqwa) is best. Let not Satan deceive you just as he had your parents expelled from Paradise; for he and his tribe see you from where you cannot see them. When hypocrites commit evil, they say: 'We found our fathers doing this, and Allah has commanded it of us.' Say: 'Allah does not command indecency; do you say against Allah that which you do not know?'\n\nAllah enjoins justice; direct your faces toward Him at every place of worship (masjid) and call upon Him, making religion purely sincere for Him alone. As He originated you, so will you return to Him. A group He guided, and a group deserved straying; for they took devils as allies instead of Allah and thought they were rightly guided. For every nation there is a term; when it arrives, they can neither delay it by an hour nor advance it.\n\nAllah commands that fine clothing be worn when going to the mosques, and that one eat and drink but not be wasteful. Wastefulness (israf) entails declaring the unlawful as lawful, making the lawful unlawful, or going to excess in eating, drinking, and adornment. Allah does not love the wasteful. As the occasion of revelation (sabab al-nuzul) of this verse, it is narrated that in the pre-Islamic period of ignorance (jahiliyyah), there was the custom of performing circumambulation (tawaf) naked, and the Banu Amir used to torment themselves by eating very little during the days of pilgrimage (Hajj). When the Muslims also wished to act likewise, this verse was revealed. In this verse, the principles of preserving health, which constitutes half of medicine, are summarized.\n\nNo one can forbid the adornments and wholesome provisions that Allah has produced for His servants. This verse is evidence that the default ruling regarding clothing and food is permissibility. Prohibition applies only to what Allah has explicitly forbidden. Allah has forbidden immoralities (both open and secret), sin, wrongful rebellion, associating partners with Him for which He has sent down no authority, and speaking about Allah without knowledge. Polytheism is sheer ignorance consisting of nothing other than following Satan without evidence.\n\nFor every community there is an appointed term (a time of destruction). When that appointed term arrives, it can neither be delayed nor advanced. This means that the disbelievers and the rebellious are not punished immediately but are granted respite, yet in the end they will certainly meet their punishment. Believers must not be deceived by this respite, but should repent immediately and adhere firmly to Allah's commands.\n\nThose who deny Our verses and act arrogantly against them are those for whom the gates of heaven will not be opened, nor will they enter Paradise. They can only enter Paradise when a camel passes through the eye of a needle; this is a proverb expressing impossibility. The disbelievers will have beds and coverings of fire in Hell. As for the believers, they will enter Paradise, rancor will be removed from their hearts, rivers will flow beneath them, and they will praise Allah.\n\nBetween the people of Paradise and the people of Hell there is a veil, and on the Heights (al-A'raf) are those who recognize both sides by their marks. They greet the people of Paradise with peace, being those who have not yet entered it though they ardently desire to enter. The people of Hell will plead with the people of Paradise for water and provision, but this has been forbidden to the disbelievers. Because they took their religion as amusement and play, and the life of this world deceived them, they will be forgotten in the Hereafter.\n\nThere are two primary views regarding the people of al-A'raf. According to the first, they are a monotheistic group whose deeds fell short and whose good deeds and misdeeds weighed equally in the Scale (mizan). They remain for a time between Paradise and Hell, after which Allah passes judgment concerning them. According to the second view, they are figures of high rank, such as prophets, martyrs, the righteous, and scholars. Under the first view, the people of al-A'raf are those who have not yet entered Paradise but desire to enter, and the people of Paradise say to them, 'Peace be upon you.' Under the second view, this situation describes the state of the people of Paradise before they enter it, and the people of al-A'raf give them glad tidings of peace and safety.\n\nWhat is described in verses 48–51 is the calling out by the people of al-A'raf to individuals they recognize by their marks. These were disbelievers such as al-Walid ibn al-Mughirah, Abu Jahl, al-'As ibn Wa'il, and their like. They used to say, 'Is Allah going to admit people like Salman and 'Ammar into Paradise while throwing us into Hell?' These are the very ones to whom the people of al-A'raf cry out, 'Neither your gathering nor your arrogance has availed you anything.' And those whom they point to, asking, 'Are these the ones?', are believers such as Salman and 'Ammar.\n\nIn verses 52–53, it is stated that Allah brought them a Book that He detailed based on knowledge, as guidance and mercy for a people who believe. The disbelievers, however, await its interpretation (ta'wil)—that is, its ultimate outcome and the realization of the news it conveyed. Ta'wil means bringing something to its conclusion. In order to believe in the Hereafter, they wait for the Day of Resurrection to arrive. When that Day comes, they will say, 'The messengers of our Lord certainly brought the truth,' and they will seek intercessors or wish to return to the worldly life, but this will be of no benefit to them.\n\nIn verse 54, it is stated that Allah created the heavens and the earth in six days, then established Himself above the Throne (istiwa' 'ala al-'Arsh). The expression 'six days' does not mean known days like worldly days, but denotes periods or spans of time. These timeframes may be shorter or longer than a known day. For instance, it is indicated in verses that a single day may last a thousand years or fifty thousand years. The true nature of these six days is allegorical (mutashabih), yet it is certain that the universe is not eternal (qadim), that it was created stage by stage, and that it is under the dominion of Allah.\n\nIn verses 55–58, it is commanded to supplicate to Allah in humility and secret, that He does not love transgressors, and that corruption should not be caused on earth. Allah, who sends the winds as glad tidings before His mercy, bears heavy clouds, drives them to a dry land, sends down rain, and brings forth all kinds of crops. In this manner the dead will also be brought forth. The vegetation of a good land emerges by the permission of its Lord, whereas from the bad land nothing comes forth except stunted, useless growth. Thus are the verses explained for a people who are grateful.\n\nThere are two main views regarding istiwa' and the Throne (al-'Arsh). According to the school of the early righteous predecessors (the Salaf), one believes in the attribute of Allah's istiwa' over the Throne while entrusting its modality (kayfiyyah) to Allah's knowledge. Later scholars (al-muta'akhkhirun), however, adopted figurative interpretation (ta'wil), understanding istiwa' as signifying Allah's majesty and sovereignty. The Throne is an exalted entity that encompasses the entire universe; whether it is a physical body (jism) is debated. Istiwa' must be understood in a manner befitting Allah, free from attributes such as place and direction.\n\nWhen explaining the phrase 'then He established His dominion over the Throne' in Surah al-A'raf, verse 54, Elmalılı Hamdi Yazır states that the concept of 'istiwa'' in Arabic is a metonymy (kinayah) for complete ownership and the orderly execution of affairs. This expression conveys the penetration of Allah's power and the unfolding of His will through His continuous command over His creation and His implementation of orderly decrees. Hasan al-Basri expressed this as 'He assumed mastery over His affair.' The word 'then' (thumma) indicates that at the initial moments of creation there was not yet an orderly continuity, whereas istiwa' can be conceived with the commencement of this order.\n\nIn the second interpretation, istiwa' is taken to mean 'istila',' that is, triumph and dominion. According to this, after creation Allah held everything under His power and mastery. This meaning is related to the expression 'He encompasses all things' in Surah as-Sajdah, verse 54. Although Ibn al-'Arabi did not accept this meaning, it was countered with the poet's line: 'Bishr conquered (istawla 'ala) Iraq without a sword and without shedding blood.'\n\nIn the third interpretation, the Throne is taken to mean the realm and dominion, while istiwa' is taken to mean mastery (istila'). This interpretation offers three benefits: first, it draws special attention to the meaning of istiwa'; second, it serves as a reminder of Allah's supreme authority over human sovereignties; third, it emphasizes Allah's sublime and absolute perfection not only in His active attributes, but in all His essential attributes. Furthermore, from this perspective, the delay implied by the word 'then' does not pertain to time between creation and istiwa', but rather to the rank of textual exposition.\n\nAccording to a fourth interpretation, 'He established Himself over the Throne' means that everything is equal in relation to Allah. That is, Allah is not like physical bodies that move from one place to another; those in the heavens and those on the earth are at an equal distance to Him. This is an understanding that eliminates the limitations of place and distance. However, this should not be interpreted as meaning that istiwa' itself signifies equality; rather, the intent is an essential or relational attribute that entails equality. This meaning can be illustrated by the example of a geometric center, yet in Allah's divine relation, no distinction of distance can be conceived.\n\nAll these interpretations are intended to prevent the word istiwa' from being understood in physical senses such as sitting or leaning. To imagine Allah sitting upon a throne or leaning against a place is, both rationally and religiously, gross ignorance. The soundest path is to follow the school of the majority of the Salaf and say, 'Allah created the heavens and the earth, then He established Himself over the Throne in the sense He intended.' However, this does not mean that the verse contains no comprehensible meaning, for the continuation of the verse concerning the covering of the night and the day elucidates this istiwa'.\n\nIn the continuation of the verse, the expression 'He wraps the night over the day, which pursues it incessantly' displays one of the broadest manifestations of istiwa. Allah drapes the night over the day; in the moment of this draping, the light of the day turns into darkness, and darkness into light. This transformation continues in a ceaseless alternation and proclaims Allah's sovereignty. The mutual draping of night and day in this manner demonstrates the existence of a will superior to their natures.\n\nThe chasing of night and day after one another is expressed with spiritual words such as 'talab' (pursuit) and 'hathith' (prompting/swiftly). This describes both an objective and a subjective state. Although the hasty pursuit of the day may appear like a pursuit of the sun, contrary to this pursuit, the night is brought and the day is vanquished. This shows that Allah has subjected the sun, the moon, and the stars to His command. None of them is an originator or a determiner on its own; all of them are subject to Allah's command.\n\nAll creation and command belong to Allah. Created beings are His dominion, and the decrees that govern them are also His. Bodies and physical forms, matter and shape are His invention; the force and spirit that animate them are His effect. Nothing can exist without being grounded in His origination, nor can there be any command that does not conform to His command. The expression 'Unquestionably, His is the creation and the command' summarizes this truth.\n\nNext, with the attribute 'Lord of the worlds', the superiority of beings possessed of intellect over other realms and the fact that Allah's greatness transcends the intellect are recalled. In their journey of knowing Allah, those endowed with intellect should neither neglect the value of their reason nor overestimate reason and imagine that sovereignty belongs to it. They should neither become fixated solely on external manifestations and see the sun as clearer than its Lord, nor turn entirely inward and suppose their Lord to be within themselves. Sovereignty belongs neither to the realm of the sun and the moon nor to the realm of intellects; it belongs to the Truth Most High (Haqq Ta'ala) above both.\n\nAllah Almighty commands His servants to supplicate to Him with humility and in secret. Here, in the recitation of 'Asim according to the transmission of Abu Bakr Shu'bah, there is also a reading that means 'with a kind of fear.' That is to say, first know your place and recognize your Lord; His greatness, majesty, and authority of creation and command belong exclusively to Him. You are in need of Him at every moment and are under His decree. Then know that you are permitted and indeed commanded to pray directly to Him. In your supplication, you should be neither reckless nor loud and clamorous; you must implore secretly with complete reverence and sincerity. For Allah does not love the transgressors. Those who exceed the limits in supplication and lose moderation in their prayers are deprived of His love.\n\nSupplication is the lesser entreating the Greater with earnestness for one's need. For this reason, a state of entreaty, sincerity, and earnestness is essential in prayer. Shouting and clamoring crosses the boundary from sincerity into ostentation. In an authentic hadith, our Prophet (peace be upon him) said: 'You are not calling upon one who is deaf or absent; rather, you are calling upon Allah, Who is All-Hearing and near.' Transgressing in supplication is like asking for supernatural feats (miracles), asking for the rank of prophethood, or asking for things that are sinful. When Abdullah ibn Mughaffal heard his son pray, 'O Allah, when I enter Paradise, I ask You for the white palace on its right side,' he warned him and related that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: 'There will be a people who will transgress in supplication.' When supplicating, one must be balanced between fear and hope.\n\nDo not cause corruption on the earth after it has been set in order. Allah created the earth, established its order, settled you upon it, and provided sustenance. Your responsibility is either to improve it or to corrupt it. Let the goal of your desires be tawhid (monotheism), faith, obedience, justice, and righteousness. Seek to build, not to destroy; pursue goodness, not corruption. A person should reflect upon the catastrophe of being deprived of the things they need and avoid every form of mischief-making. When praying, be in a state of both fear and hope; fear and hope are like two wings on the spiritual path. Those who maintain this balance become among the muhsinun (the doers of good) who have attained the station of ihsan. Allah does not let the reward of those who do good go to waste.\n\nAllah sends winds as heralds of glad tidings of His mercy. According to recitation variants, it is read as 'nashirat' or 'nushr'. These winds stir the stagnant air when it is about to rain, making motion the precursor to blessing. Wind is of eight kinds: four are punishment, and four are mercy. The winds of mercy are the nashirat, the mubashshirat, the mursalat, and the dhariyat. The movement of the winds is a divine dispensation. Indeed, they lift and carry heavy clouds. This is also noteworthy from the standpoint of physical science; heavy water droplets are borne by the buoyant force of air. This situation is an analogy for the resurrection of the dead. Allah sends down water upon a dead land and brings forth fruits with it; in like manner will the dead be raised.\n\nA good land brings forth its vegetation richly by the permission of its Lord, while that which is bad yields only with difficulty and is useless. This is a parable for mankind. Prophets and divine messages are a mercy like rain. Human beings, too, are like the soil, divided into two categories: the good and the bad. The good benefit from the prophets, take heed, believe, and produce righteous deeds. The wicked, on the other hand, deny the blessings and remain deprived. Thus do We explain the verses in diverse ways for a people who give thanks.\n\nNoah (Nuh) was sent as a prophet to his people. He told them to worship Allah and said that he feared for them the punishment of a tremendous day. The notables of the people accused him of clear misguidance. Noah declared that he was a messenger sent by the Lord of the worlds and that there was no misguidance in him. They denied him; so We saved Noah and those with him in the ark, and drowned those who denied Our verses. Then, to the people of 'Ad, We sent their brother Hud. He, too, called them to tawhid (monotheism). His people accused him of foolishness and lying. Hud reminded them of Allah's blessings and warned them of punishment. When they insisted on not abandoning the idols of their forefathers, the punishment came, and those who believed were saved.\n\nAnd to the people of Thamud, We sent their brother Salih. He brought the she-camel of Allah as a miracle and told them not to harm her. Those who grew arrogant among the people transgressed the command of their Lord by slaughtering the she-camel; then the earthquake seized them and destroyed them. Afterwards, We sent the prophet Lot (Lut). His people were committing a grave obscenity by leaving women and turning to men. They sought to expel Lot and his followers. We saved Lot and his family, while his wife remained with the disbelievers. We rained down upon them a rain of punishment. And to Midian (Madyan), We sent Shu'ayb. He forbade cheating in measure and weight and causing corruption on the earth. A part of his people believed, while another part sat on every road threatening and hindering people from the way of Allah. Shu'ayb told them to await Allah's judgment. These stories clarify historical truths that had been corrupted before the revelation of the Quran and provide an admonition for humanity.\n\nElmalılı Hamdi Yazır addresses the purpose behind the narration of Quranic stories and the accounts transmitted by commentators concerning them. He states that some commentators, especially earlier ones, transmitted narrations circulating prior to the Quran, thereby demonstrating how the Quran corrected the corruptions within those narrations. However, he notes that those lacking expertise in tafsir perceived these transmissions as the very essence of the stories, straying from the path of Quranic truth, and seeking religion in bizarre interpretations outside the Sunnah. In response to them, he expresses that pure naturalists dismissed these accounts as 'fables of the ancients' or attributed them entirely to nature. The Quran, on the other hand, emphasizes that the truth lies between these two extremes and conveys the stories in the most beautiful manner. He indicates that these stories must be read with careful attention to the essence of each prophet's call, their method of conveying the message, their debates with their people, the outcomes of faith and disbelief, and shared values. He affirms that all causes of downfall and destruction stem from failing to heed the command of the Truth (al-Haqq), failing to appreciate the leaders sent by Allah, and ingratitude. He adds that the true religion is not a human-made social institution, but a divine establishment, and that the capacity of every nation for life and happiness is in harmony with the glory of the Creator.\n\nAgainst the preaching of Shu'ayb (peace be upon him), the arrogant notables among his people threaten to either expel him and the believers from their land or force them to return to their religion. Shu'ayb (peace be upon him) replies that returning to their religion after Allah had delivered them from disbelief would be fabricating a lie against Allah, and that this would not be possible unless Allah so willed. He states that they rely upon Allah and prays for Him to judge between them with justice. The disbelievers among the people, however, declare that those who follow Shu'ayb will surely be in ruin. Then the dreadful earthquake seizes them, and they are left fallen prone upon their knees in their dwellings. Those who denied Shu'ayb became as though they had never prospered there. Shu'ayb (peace be upon him) turns away from them, declaring that he had conveyed the truths sent by his Lord and had advised them, asking how he could now grieve over a disbelieving people. Elmalılı explains the word 'juthum' (juthum): it means collapsing and clinging to the ground chest-first while grasping both legs with the hands, just as a hare or bird sleeps. That is, they stumbled and fell prone upon their faces in their homeland such that not a trace of movement remained in them. He also notes that Shu'ayb felt sorrow over the destruction of his people, but, recognizing that they were not worthy of grief, severed his spiritual concern, neither gloating over their fate nor finding it permissible to lament for them.\n\nTo explain that the nations uprooted by this severe punishment deserved such an outcome, and to present a concise summary of the cautionary lessons from these five stories before moving to the sixth, the following verses were revealed: 'We never sent a prophet to any town but We seized its people with poverty and hardship so that they might humble themselves. Then We exchanged their suffering for good (prosperity), until they multiplied and said: 'Our fathers too were touched by hardship and joy.' So We seized them suddenly while they were unaware.' In his commentary on these verses, Elmalılı explains that the people of every town to which a prophet was sent were first afflicted with poverty and illness, and then granted abundance and health, thereby being tested with both bitter and sweet circumstances. This continued until they multiplied—that is, increased in wealth and population. By saying, 'Our fathers too were touched by hardship and joy,' they failed to recognize that these conditions stemmed from Allah's discipline, regarding them instead as natural and inevitable occurrences. It was then that We seized them suddenly. Whether the seizure being sudden means the destruction occurred instantaneously or unfolded over a period makes no difference; what matters is vigilance before the seizure begins.\n\nContinuing: 'And if the people of those towns had believed and feared (Allah's punishment), We would certainly have opened for them blessings from the heaven and the earth; but they denied, so We seized them for what they used to earn.' Elmalılı notes that in this verse, faith and piety (taqwa) bring blessings, whereas denial leads to punishment. In the verses that follow, it is questioned whether the people of the towns felt secure that punishment would not come upon them while they slept at night or played in the forenoon. It is emphasized that none feel secure from Allah's plan (makr) except the people who are in loss. Elmalılı explains that the expression 'Allah's plan' is a metaphor signifying that He punishes His servants gradually and from an unexpected quarter. Furthermore, it is stated that it has become clear to those who inherit the earth after its previous inhabitants that if He had willed, He could have afflicted them too for their sins, but He seals their hearts so they do not hear the truth. Thus are some of the news of those cities narrated; their messengers had brought them clear proofs, but they were not going to believe in the truths they had previously denied. Thus does Allah seal the hearts of the disbelievers. We did not find most of them keeping their word; rather, We found most of them to be defiantly disobedient.\n\nThe story of Moses (Musa) is recounted in greater detail than the others because his miracles were more potent and the ignorance and tyranny of his people were more pervasive. Elmalılı dwells in detail on the meaning of the word 'mala'' (mala'). 'Mala'' denotes a council or assembly united on a common opinion, whose form and appearance fill the eyes, and whose stature and importance captivate hearts. In modern terms, this corresponds to 'high society' or the ruling elite. This council gathered around Pharaoh represented the entirety of the people. Moses was sent with signs to Pharaoh and this council of his. They, however, wronged those signs, treating them as lies and rejecting them. Moses came to Pharaoh and said, 'I am a messenger from the Lord of the worlds; it is an obligation upon me to speak nothing concerning Allah except the truth. I have come to you with a clear proof from your Lord; so send the Children of Israel with me.' Pharaoh demanded a miracle, saying, 'If you are of the truthful, then bring it forth!' Moses threw down his staff, and it became an enormous serpent. According to narration, when the serpent began slithering toward Pharaoh, Pharaoh grew terrified and pleaded, whereupon Moses took hold of it. Elmalılı explains that this transformation was not an overturning of essential reality, but rather a change in the form of the staff's matter, or the placement of the serpent's form in the mind in its stead. When Moses drew forth his hand from his bosom, it turned radiant white, shining brightly. These two miracles are manifestations of Allah's wrath and mercy.\n\nThe council (mala') of Pharaoh said, 'This is surely a very knowledgeable sorcerer; he wants to drive you out of your land. What do you instruct?' Elmalılı notes that the phrase 'what do you instruct?' is remarkably striking; this indicates that the governing authority of Pharaoh's regime rested in the hands of this consultative council. Furthermore, the statement 'He wants to drive you out of your land' suggests that the council was composed of the native population of Egypt. Some Quranic commentators have even posited that the matter was submitted to a public referendum. Elmalılı emphasizes that Pharaoh temporarily set aside his claim to divinity here, displaying a hypocritical humility by asking 'what do you instruct?', which represents a prominent example of Pharaoh's own sorcery. Moreover, his demand for a miracle from Moses indicates that Pharaoh was already aware that truthful prophets had come in the past and brought miracles.\n\nPharaoh's consultative council did not immediately launch an attack against the miracles of Moses (Musa), but instead proposed holding a contest. They said to him, \"Stall Moses and his brother for a while, send heralds to the cities, and gather all the skilled magicians.\" This proposal demonstrates that mutual consultation (mushawarah and istisharah) is beneficial in bringing the truth to light. It also indicates that Aaron (Harun) was alongside Moses.\n\nThe assembled magicians put on a great display of magic, bewitching the eyes of the people and presenting terrifying illusions. They cast down thick ropes and staffs, making the valley appear full of snakes. According to narration, this was an optical illusion accomplished with hollow pieces of wood and leather filled with mercury, which moved when heated. However, Allah revealed to Moses to cast down his staff, and the staff swallowed all of their fabrications. Thus, the truth became manifest and falsehood vanished.\n\nFaced with this event, the magicians, knowing the true limits of sorcery, realized that this was a divine miracle and immediately fell down in prostration, accepting faith. They said: \"We believe in the Lord of the worlds, the Lord of Moses and Aaron.\" This demonstrates the value of knowledge and expertise: even expertise in the craft of magic enabled them to distinguish a miracle from sorcery. This shows what great benefits expertise in the doctrine of tawhid (the oneness of God) can offer humanity.\n\nPharaoh was humiliated by the magicians' embracing faith and concocted an intrigue to mislead public opinion. Rebuking them, he said: \"Did you believe in him before I gave you permission? This is surely a plot; you conspired with Moses in the city to drive out its people from Egypt.\" Yet the reality was the sincere faith of the magicians in the face of the overwhelming power of the miracle. Pharaoh attempted to exploit patriotic sentiments to portray Moses and the believers as enemies.\n\nScholars have stated that these verses are among the greatest proofs regarding the virtue of knowledge. Because the magicians understood the essence of sorcery, they immediately realized that Moses' staff was not magic. Had they been ignorant, they might have fallen into doubt, thinking, \"Perhaps he simply knows magic better than we do.\" Instead, they believed without hesitation. This shows that specialization in any field is an important means for recognizing the truth.\n\nSome who wish to deny the reality of the miracles mentioned in the verses have interpreted them as metaphors and allegories. However, this stems from being entrenched in naturalistic assumptions and thereby denying miracles. Yet once reason verifies the authenticity of a transmitted report, if it is conveyed as an extraordinary occurrence, reason should not judge it merely by its own conventional standards, but investigate its intrinsic possibility. The power of Allah is limitless; He can do whatever He wills. To claim that extraordinary events are impossible is also contrary to the scientific mindset, for science does not deny rare occurrences; it merely generalizes.\n\nFakhr al-Din al-Razi relates that there are three views regarding whether a departure from customary norms (khariq al-adah) is possible: the Ahl al-Sunnah generally regard it as possible; naturalist philosophers deem it impossible; and the Mu'tazilah accept part of it as possible and part as impossible. Naturalists claim that acknowledging extraordinary occurrences invalidates scientific causality. However, this claim is inconsistent, because whether without a cause or through causes, the possibility of extraordinary events is inevitable in every case. Allah, the All-Knowing and All-Powerful, creates whatever He wills.\n\nIn modern philosophy, the certainty of natural sciences has been debated, and Kant's view has been presented as a middle path. According to Kant, because the laws of nature are derived from experience, they are not strictly necessary but merely report what has occurred. This view is compatible with the approach of Muslim theologians (mutakallimun).\n\nThe possibility of miracles is no more extraordinary than the creation and destruction of the universe. Customary events (adah) fall within our certain knowledge, yet the possibility of the contrary is likewise within our knowledge. For example, that a snake comes from another snake is certain, but its creation from something else is also entirely possible.\n\nEvents in the universe operate through the balance of two opposing harmonies: positive and negative. All recognition and differentiation occur by virtue of a departure from the customary (an extraordinary occurrence). Miracles are also of these distinguishing characteristics and constitute a divine custom (adah ilahiyyah). They are known not through pure reason and analogy, but through observation and transmitted reports.\n\nSome deny miracles by saying, \"We are more advanced than the ancients; we can do what they could not do.\" This is an obstinate attitude that harms science and progress. They fail to see the variability of nature and that it begins anew with fresh creation at every moment. For example, it is normal for a staff to decay and change over time; for that same transformation to happen instantaneously is by no means impossible.\n\nThe Quran draws attention to normal customs, but it does not deny miracles. One must not place limits on Allah's power. In verse 7:54, it is emphasized that both creation and command belong to Allah. The stories of the prophets should be understood through the exemplar of this initial creation; the hearts of those who deny are sealed.\n\nThe laws of nature are formed through observation, experimentation, and generalization, but they are not absolute. Our knowledge concerning causes is incomplete and relative. For something to be deemed impossible, a genuine logical contradiction is required. For example, instantaneous creation—much like an electric current suddenly dropping to zero—is possible. Rigid naturalists fail to see these possibilities and fall into pessimism.\n\nPharaoh, assuming Moses' miracle was sorcery, gathered the magicians. When the magicians were defeated, they embraced faith. Pharaoh threatened them, saying, \"I will surely cut off your hands and your feet on opposite sides and crucify you.\" The magicians replied, \"Indeed, to our Lord we will return,\" and remained steadfast in their faith. They declared that Pharaoh was seeking revenge against them solely because they had believed.\n\nThe magicians pointed out that death is inevitable, that martyrdom is a reward, and that Pharaoh himself would also die. They prayed to Allah for patience and to die as Muslims. This event made it absolute that the miracle was not magic. Some scholars state that the magicians were martyred, and the flow of the verses supports this.\n\nThe notables among Pharaoh's people incited him against Moses and the Children of Israel. They said, \"If you leave them, they will cause corruption in the land and abandon you and your gods.\" Here, the term 'gods' refers to the deities that Pharaoh approved of and permitted. In reality, Pharaoh considered himself the supreme lord.\n\nPharaoh was gripped by immense fear in the face of Moses' staff miracle. To conceal this fear and boost his men's morale, without even uttering Moses' name, he attempted to dispel their anxieties by claiming, 'We will kill the male children of the Children of Israel and spare their women; indeed, we hold overwhelming dominance over them.' In fact, he was so terrified of Moses that he did not even dare utter his name. Through these words, he implied that Moses' strength derived from his people, claiming he could crush and destroy them.\n\nWhen Moses saw that his people were thrown into panic by Pharaoh's threats, he gave them two commands and two glad tidings: Seek help from Allah and be patient. For the earth belongs to Allah; He gives it as an inheritance to whom He wills of His servants. The favorable outcome belongs to the righteous who fear Allah. If you too protect yourselves (through piety), the ultimate outcome will be yours. With these words, he sought to reassure his people.\n\nThe Children of Israel, however, complained despite the comfort offered by Moses (Musa), saying, 'We were suffering persecution before you came to us, and we are still suffering after you have come.' These words reflected both the pain felt under Pharaoh's oppression and a longing for deliverance. Moses gave them glad tidings, saying, 'Perhaps your Lord will destroy your enemy, make you successors in the land, and see how you act.'\n\nTo warn Pharaoh and his people, Allah afflicted them with years of famine and hardship. Whenever good came to them, they said, 'This is our due,' but when evil befell them, they blamed Moses and those with him as an evil omen. Thereupon, Allah sent upon them distinct signs such as the flood, locusts, pests, frogs, and blood. Each time, they pleaded with Moses and promised that they would believe, but as soon as the punishment was lifted, they broke their word.\n\nIn the end, Allah took retribution upon them and drowned them all in the sea. They deserved this punishment because they denied His verses and were heedless of them. As for the Children of Israel who had been oppressed and despised, He made them heirs to the blessed eastern and western parts of the land. Your Lord's gracious promise to them was fulfilled because of their patience. And He destroyed what Pharaoh and his people had constructed and erected.\n\nAfter the Children of Israel crossed the sea, they came upon a people devoted to idols and said to Moses, 'Make for us a god just as they have gods.' Moses rebuked them for their ignorance, replying, 'Indeed, the religion these people follow is doomed to destruction, and what they do is futile. Shall I seek for you a god other than Allah, when He has favored you above all the worlds?'\n\nWhen Allah delivered the Children of Israel from the oppression of Pharaoh, He put them through a great trial. Pharaoh was inflicting the worst torment upon them by slaughtering their sons and sparing their daughters.\n\nWhen the Children of Israel encountered a people worshipping idols, they asked Moses to make similar idols for them. Moses told them that they were acting out of ignorance and reminded them that the religion of the idolaters was false and that Allah had favored them above all the worlds.\n\nMoses emphasized to his people that seeking a deity other than Allah was ingratitude. Reminding them of their deliverance from Pharaoh, he stated that they ought to be grateful for these blessings.\n\nMoses was appointed thirty nights, and ten nights were added to them, completing forty nights. Leaving his brother Aaron (Harun) as his deputy over his people, Moses went to the appointment set by Allah. During this period, purification was attained through fasting and worship.\n\nAt the appointed time, Moses was blessed with the speech of Allah. However, upon his desire to see Allah, he was told, \"You will never see Me\"; and when Allah manifested His glory to the mountain, it crumbled to dust, and Moses fell unconscious. When he recovered, he repented and declared that he was the first of the believers.\n\nAllah wrote for Moses on the Tablets concerning all things, commanding him to adhere firmly to them and instructing his people to take the best of them. Furthermore, it was conveyed that those who behave arrogantly on earth would be turned away from His verses, and that the deeds of those who deny the verses and the Hereafter would come to nothing.\n\nIn Moses' absence, the Children of Israel fashioned a statue of a calf from ornaments that made a lowing sound, and they worshipped it. When they were stricken with remorse and felt deep regret, they said, \"If our Lord does not have mercy upon us, we shall surely be among the losers.\" When Moses returned angry and grieved, he put down the Tablets and questioned his brother Aaron. Aaron explained that the people deemed him weak and almost killed him. Moses prayed to Allah to forgive him and his brother. It was revealed that those who worshipped the calf would face wrath and humiliation, but those who repented would be forgiven. Then Moses took up the Tablets; inscribed upon them were guidance and mercy for those who fear their Lord.\n\nIn verse 151, Aaron (peace be upon him) defends himself in an eloquent manner to appease the anger of his brother Moses, explaining that he is innocent. Moses then asks Allah's forgiveness first for himself and then for his brother; this indicates that Aaron too was in need of seeking forgiveness (istighfar).\n\nIn verse 152, it is stated that those who took the calf as a deity will suffer humiliation in this world and divine wrath in the Hereafter. As for the punishment of al-Samiri, it was the affliction of 'do not touch'. This punishment serves as a general warning for those who invent lies against Allah and is not exclusive to the Children of Israel.\n\nIn verse 153, it is emphasized that for those who commit evil deeds and then repent and believe, Allah is Forgiving and Merciful. The door of repentance is open and Allah fulfills His promise; this stands as a source of hope alongside the warnings in the preceding verses.\n\nIn verse 154, when Moses' anger subsides, he takes up the Tablets again. In these Tablets are guidance and mercy; however, this is for those who fear Allah. The taking up of the Tablets became possible once Moses had calmed down.\n\nIn verse 155, Moses chooses seventy men from his people and takes them to Mount Sinai (Tur Sina) for repentance at the appointed time. However, when they say they will not believe until they see Allah, they are seized by a violent earthquake. Moses states that this is a trial and asks Allah for forgiveness.\n\nIn verse 156, Moses asks for good in this world and the Hereafter. Allah declares that His punishment strikes whom He wills, while His mercy encompasses all things. Yet this mercy is specifically ordained for those who possess piety (taqwa), give zakah, and believe in His verses. This demonstrates that mercy will subsequently be specified for them.\n\nIn verse 157, the characteristics of the unlettered Prophet (al-nabiyy al-ummi) are described: he enjoins good, forbids evil, makes lawful what is wholesome, and prohibits what is foul. He relieves them of their heavy burdens and the shackles that bound them. This Prophet is found written in the Torah and the Gospel and is recognized by his attributes.\n\nThose who believe in that Prophet, honor him, support him, and follow the light sent down with him are the ones who attain salvation. This mercy is addressed not only to the Children of Israel, but to all of humanity, and has been decreed for the ummah of the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him).\n\nAllah the Almighty conveys that in the story of Moses, the premises of mercy and punishment are distinguished from one another, and with the prophethood of the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him), mercy has become a universal principle. Mercy is now open not merely to a single people, but to all mankind. Indeed, it has been revealed: \"Say: O My servants who have transgressed against themselves, do not despair of the mercy of Allah!\" (az-Zumar 39:53). Moses (peace be upon him), at the appointed time and place (miqat) on Mount Sinai, requested this dispensation of mercy for himself and his people, but Allah declared that this would only be possible by following the Prophet of the end times and his law (Shari'ah). Thus, the story of Moses points toward the coming Prophet of mercy.\n\nAt this point, there is a manifestation of the divine istiwa mentioned in the verse: \"Indeed, your Lord is Allah, who created the heavens and the earth in six days and then established Himself over the Throne\" (al-A'raf, 7:54). As stated in the noble hadith: \"Time has revolved back to its shape on the day Allah created the heavens and the earth.\" With the era of the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him), an entirely new epoch of history commenced, and the dispensations of previous prophets merged within this universal order. The descriptions in the Torah and the Gospel likewise corroborate this reality, demonstrating that Moses was a prophet sent solely to the Children of Israel, preparing them for the Prophet who was to come.\n\nFor this reason, Allah Almighty commands the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) as follows: \"Say: O mankind! Indeed, I am the Messenger of Allah to you all. To Him belongs the dominion of the heavens and the earth. There is no deity except Him. He gives life and causes death. So believe in Allah and His unlettered Prophet—who believes in Allah and all His words—and follow him so that you may be guided.\" (Al-A'raf, 7/158) This verse clearly proclaims that the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) was sent to all of humanity. The claims of the Jews that \"Muhammad was sent only to the Arabs\" are groundless and consist merely of distorting the Torah.\n\nThen Allah informs that among the people of Moses (Musa) there was a community that guided to the right path: \"And among the people of Moses is a community which guides by truth and by it establishes justice.\" (Al-A'raf, 7/159) They were people who followed the truth and judged with justice. Allah divided them into twelve tribes and gave a separate spring to each of them. He also made clouds a shade over them and sent down manna and quails. Yet they showed ingratitude for these blessings and wronged themselves. Afterwards, Allah commanded them to settle in a town and say 'hittah' (forgiveness), but the wrongdoers among them altered this word to say 'hintah' (wheat). Thereupon, Allah sent down a punishment from heaven upon them.\n\nAllah reminds the Children of Israel of what befell the town by the seashore. The people of that town were violating the Sabbath prohibition and fishing. On the Sabbath, the fish would come in shoals, whereas on other days they would not come. A group among them said to those who warned against this evil, \"Why do you admonish a people whom Allah will destroy?\" Those who warned replied, \"To be an excuse before your Lord and so that perhaps they may avoid evil.\" Ultimately, when that people forgot the admonitions, those who warned were saved, while the wrongdoers were subjected to a severe punishment. When they exceeded all bounds in what was forbidden, it was said to them, \"Be despised apes!\"\n\nAllah informs that until the Day of Resurrection, He will send against the Children of Israel those who will inflict upon them the worst punishment. Indeed, by sending Nebuchadnezzar (Bukht Nasr) against them, He ruined their state and condemned them to heavy taxes. Nonetheless, Allah is Most Forgiving, Most Merciful. Then He dispersed them across the earth in fragments, dividing them into different communities, righteous and wicked. There are good among them, and there are bad. He tested them with blessings and tribulations so that perhaps they might return to the truth.\n\nAfterwards, there succeeded a corrupt generation who inherited the Book (the Torah). Saying, \"We will surely be forgiven anyway,\" they coveted worldly goods and took bribes. Yet the covenant had been taken from them that they would say nothing about Allah except the truth, and they had read and studied the Book. Allah emphasizes that the home of the Hereafter is better for those who fear Him, and proclaims that the reward of those who hold fast to the Book and establish prayer will not be lost.\n\nIn verses 169–170, mention is made of a corrupt generation that succeeded the Children of Israel. They inherited the Torah, read it, and attained the position of passing judgment. However, they did not hold fast to the Book; they used it for their own interests. In exchange for worldly gain, they would alter the truth, hope that their sins would be forgiven, and when the same gain presented itself again, they would take it once more. Yet a pledge had been taken from them to abide by the rulings in the Book. The home of the Hereafter is better for the righteous. As for those who hold fast to the Book and perform prayer, they will not lose their reward.\n\nIn verse 171, those who did not hold firmly to the Book given to the Children of Israel are reminded of Mount Sinai (Tur) being raised over them as if it were a canopy, about to fall upon them. Allah commanded them: 'Hold firmly to what We have given you and remember what is in it so that you may be protected.' This was a covenant taken under compulsion. The raising of the mountain shows Allah's power and the consequence of defying His command. People should obey the truth willingly, without having to be forced to submit. Natural phenomena are also signs of Allah's power and warn mankind.\n\nIn verses 172–174, it is related that Allah brought forth their descendants from the Children of Adam, made them bear witness against themselves, and asked, 'Am I not your Lord?' to which they replied, 'Yes, we testify.' This was so that on the Day of Resurrection they could not say, 'We were unaware of this.' There are two views regarding the nature of this covenant (mithaq): First, that it is an allegory (tamthil); Allah created human beings with an innate disposition (fitrah) inclined toward monotheism (tawhid), enabling them to recognize Him through intellect and proofs. Second, that it is an actual event; a covenant concluded in the realm of souls. Yet both views emphasize that the capacity to recognize Allah exists in the creation of man and that he must remain faithful to this covenant. This covenant is a primordial pact and forms the foundation of human responsibility.\n\nIn verses 175–176, the story is told of a man who, although given verses/signs, stripped them away and followed Satan. Had Allah willed, He would have elevated him, but he clung to the earth. His condition is like that of a dog: whether you drive it away or leave it alone, it lolls out its tongue and pants. This is the parable of the people who deny the verses/signs. This story is recounted so that a lesson may be drawn.\n\nIn verses 177–181, it is declared how wretched the condition of those who deny the verses/signs is, that whoever Allah guides is rightly guided, and whoever He leaves to stray is in ruin. It is stated that many of the jinn and mankind were created for Hell, because although they have hearts, eyes, and ears, they do not use them, and they are even lower than cattle. The most beautiful names belong to Allah; so call upon Him by them, and leave those who deviate regarding His names. Likewise, among mankind there is a community that guides to the truth and establishes justice.\n\nIn this section, the covenant (mithaq) that Allah took from human beings is dwelt upon. This covenant is an inherent commitment that human beings bring with them from creation. The progeny taken from the loins of Adam acknowledged their own existence and the oneness of their Lord. This commitment exists in the innate disposition (fitrah) of every human being and becomes conscious upon reaching puberty. While some state that this covenant was taken in the realm of souls, others say it takes place upon reaching puberty. What is fundamental, however, is that every person feels this commitment within themselves and bears responsibility.\n\nThere are different views regarding the covenant (mithaq). According to one view, this commitment begins when the progeny is taken from the father's loins and is completed through the stages passed in the mother's womb. Puberty is the time of the actual execution of this commitment. Another view contends that the covenant is taken upon reaching puberty. The expressions \"ahdh\" (taking) and \"ishhad\" (making testify) in the verse indicate the moment when a person begins to gain self-awareness. Both interpretations form the foundation of man's accountability before Allah.\n\nBy reminding them of this covenant, Allah prevented people from putting forward excuses on the Day of Resurrection, such as \"we were unaware of this\" or \"our forefathers associated partners with Allah, and we were only descendants after them.\" Every individual is responsible for their own commitment, and the sin of forefathers cannot be placed upon another. This explanation rejects blind imitation and the evasion of responsibility.\n\nThen, the story of a person who, despite having been given verses, stripped himself of them and followed Satan is related. Following worldly love and his desires, this person debased himself and remained in continuous distress, like the panting of a dog. This story represents the state of those who deny the verses. For those who plunge into debasement, it is the same whether they are warned or not.\n\nThose who deny Allah's verses are drawn step by step toward their downfall through istidraj (gradual enticement to ruin) from a place they do not know. Istidraj is a person assuming blessings are in their favor, whereas in reality they cause an increase in their punishment. This is a severe trap. Furthermore, the polytheists are criticized for not reflecting that there is no madness in the prophet, for not looking into the dominion (malakut) of the heavens and the earth, and for not realizing that their appointed time has drawn near. As for the timing of the Day of Judgment, it is known only with Allah.\n\nIn verse 187, the polytheists ask the Prophet (peace be upon him) when the Day of Judgment will take place. According to two separate narrations, in one it was the Jews and in the other the Quraysh who asked this question. Allah informs that knowledge of the Hour's time belongs solely to Him, that it will come suddenly, and that most people do not know this. These questions are asked as though with the impression that the Prophet must certainly know it, yet the answer is clear: that knowledge is with Allah.\n\nIn verse 188, the Prophet (peace be upon him) expresses that he possesses no power to bring benefit or harm to himself, but can only do as much as Allah wills. Had he known the unseen (ghayb), he would have done more good and no harm would have touched him. He is only a warner and a bearer of glad tidings. Here, it is emphasized that knowledge and power are distinct things; knowing the unseen does not entail divine lordship.\n\nIn verses 189–190, it is related that man was created from a single soul and that his mate was created from it. This soul is Adam, and his mate is Eve. When the couple come together, the woman bears a light burden and becomes pregnant. As childbirth approaches, they pray to Allah for a righteous child. But when Allah grants them a righteous child, they begin to associate partners with Him concerning this blessing. This shows that polytheism (shirk) emerged later. Most people forget this truth and follow the way of their forefathers.\n\nIn verses 191–195, it is stated that the idols associated as partners with Allah cannot create anything, are themselves created, and can help neither themselves nor those who worship them. If you call them to the right path, they do not hear; calling them and remaining silent are equal. What the polytheists worship are servants just like themselves. They do not have feet to walk with, hands to grasp with, eyes to see with, or ears to hear with. The Prophet (peace be upon him) is told that the polytheists and their partners may set traps, but he is under Allah's protection.\n\nIn verses 196–198, it is emphasized that the guardian (wali—helper and protector) of the Prophet (peace be upon him) is Allah. Allah is the One Who sent down the Book, and He protects His righteous servants. Those worshipped besides Allah, however, can neither help you nor themselves. If you call them to guidance, they do not hear; you see them looking at you, yet they do not see. In fact, in addition to the idols, these verses also encompass the leaders of the polytheists; they, too, do not hear or see in any real sense.\n\nIn verse 199, the Prophet (peace be upon him) is commanded to treat with pardon, to enjoin customary good (urf), and to turn away from the ignorant. 'Urf' refers to necessary and beneficial deeds that people recognize and accept as good. However, not every custom is urf; invalid traditions are not enjoined. Enjoining urf requires that the commanded deed be recognized good (maruf—good and beneficial). Here, it is recalled that the fundamental principles of Islam were first explained and then commanded.\n\nIn verses 200–206, it is commanded to seek refuge in Allah whenever a whisper comes from Satan. Those who fear Allah pause and reflect at the moment of whisper, retaining their insight. The brethren of the devils, on the other hand, persist in error. When a verse is not brought to the Prophet (peace be upon him), the polytheists say, 'Why did you not piece it together?'; he replies that he only follows revelation. The Quran is insights from the Lord, a guidance, and a mercy. When the Quran is recited, one should remain silent and listen, and Allah should be remembered morning and evening. The angels also prostrate before Allah and do not behave arrogantly.\n\nVerse 199 puts forward a broad principle covering areas such as morality, law, and governance. According to this principle, one must turn away from the ignorant and not respond in kind to their foolish words. Serving as an interpretation of this verse, it is stated in a hadith qudsi: 'The highest of virtues is that you seek out the one who cuts you off, bestow favors upon the one who deprives you, and forgive the one who wrongs you.' According to narration, when this verse was revealed, our Prophet (peace be upon him) asked, 'How can this be when wrath has occurred?', whereupon verse 200 was revealed.\n\nIn verses 200–201, it is commanded to immediately seek refuge in Allah in the event of an instigation from Satan. Allah hears and knows; seeking refuge inwardly in the heart is also sufficient. The God-fearing (muttaqin), even when the slightest phantom from Satan touches them, pause to think, remember, and take heed. While this verse is an address to the Messenger of Allah, it is a general warning to all believers.\n\nIn verses 202–203, those who are brothers to the devils (devils among humans and jinn) are described. They assist one another in temptation and cannot desist from evil. When you do not bring a verse to the Prophet, they malign his prophethood by saying, 'So you couldn't put it together, eh?' The Messenger of Allah [was told]: Say, 'I only follow what is revealed to me from my Lord.' This Quran is insights from your Lord, a guidance, and a mercy.\n\nIn verse 204, when the Quran is recited, listening to it and remaining silent is commanded, so that mercy may be attained. This command applies in prayer, in the sermon (khutbah), or in any gathering. Listening and remaining silent are made conditional upon recitation (qira'ah). Recitation is the audible reading by an intelligent person aimed at comprehension. For this reason, the sound coming from a gramophone or radio is not recitation, but an echo; listening to it is not obligatory (wajib).\n\nWhile listening to the Quran played on a gramophone is not obligatory, it should not be thought of as though not listening to it were obligatory. For even if it is not a complete recitation, it is similar to recitation, and disrespect is not permissible. Even though playing the Quran in an unworthy place is a sin, if it has been played, listening to it earns reward. In such a case, a Muslim should not show disrespect and, if possible, should remove it to a suitable place.\n\nIn verse 205, our Prophet (peace be upon him) and his community are commanded to remember their Lord within the soul and inwardly. This remembrance (dhikr) must be done with humility and fear, imploringly, in heart and mind, quietly, morning and evening. One must not be among the heedless, must keep the heart awake, and must continue remembering Allah. Remembrance should be in such a way that it does not hinder sound reflection.\n\nThere is a wondrous relationship between the soul and the body in human beings. The effect in the soul reflects upon the body, and the effect in the body reflects upon the soul. For example, when one imagines something sour, the mouth waters. When remembrance with the tongue becomes a habit, an impression is formed in the realm of imagination, a light rises from the soul, and this light is reflected back onto the tongue. This mutual interaction is endless; the journey of gnosis (marifah) progresses toward the pleasure of the Truth (al-Haqq), its vessel being the self (nafs) and its rudder remembrance (dhikr). Heedlessness is absolute danger.\n\nIn verse 206, those in the presence of your Lord (the angels) do not show arrogance in worshipping Him; they glorify and praise Him, and they prostrate. When this verse is recited, performing the prostration of recitation (tilawah) is prescribed. As narrated from our Prophet (peace be upon him): 'When the son of Adam recites the verse of prostration and prostrates, Satan withdraws weeping and says, \"Woe to me; I was commanded to prostrate and I rebelled, so for me is the Fire.\"' Through this remembrance and prostration, a path is opened from al-A'raf to Paradise."